{% comment %}
  Turns a section's two universal settings — ground and vertical rhythm — into
  a class string. Capture it, don't render it inline:

    {%- capture band -%}
      {%- render 'sb-section-classes', rhythm: section.settings.rhythm -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}
    <section data-surface="{{ section.settings.surface }}" class="bg-background text-foreground {{ band }}">

  WHY A SNIPPET AND NOT STRING CONCATENATION: Tailwind emits only the utilities
  it can find as literal text while scanning. `section-{{ setting }}` is invisible
  to it, and the class would be missing from the built stylesheet — the classic
  way a themed setting silently does nothing. Writing all five out here, in a
  case statement, is what keeps them in the build.

  The `section-*` utilities rebind --sb-section for everything inside the band,
  so a nested `py-section` follows without a per-element override. That is the
  same trick data-surface plays with colour.

  Accepts:
    rhythm — xs | sm | md | lg | xl | theme (default: theme, i.e. inherit the
             global setting rather than pinning this band to a size)
    pad    — none | y (default: y, which applies py-section)
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  case rhythm
    when 'xs'
      assign rhythm_class = 'section-xs'
    when 'sm'
      assign rhythm_class = 'section-sm'
    when 'md'
      assign rhythm_class = 'section-md'
    when 'lg'
      assign rhythm_class = 'section-lg'
    when 'xl'
      assign rhythm_class = 'section-xl'
    else
      assign rhythm_class = ''
  endcase

  if pad == 'none'
    assign pad_class = ''
  else
    assign pad_class = 'py-section'
  endif
-%}

{{- rhythm_class }} {{ pad_class -}}
